   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to the study of the utilization of state government\npayments as an opportunity to transition unbanked and underbanked state\nresidents into the banking system",
    "basePrintNo": "S5669",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5669-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Banks",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S5669\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to the study of the utilization of state government\npayments as an opportunity to transition unbanked and underbanked state\nresidents into the banking system\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would require a study by the Department of Financial Services\nof the utilization of state government payments as an opportunity to\ntransition unbanked and underbanked state residents into the banking.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. The superintendent of the department of financial services,\nin conjunction with the director of the state budget, the director of\nstate operations and the commissioner of the department of taxation and\nfinance, and in consultation with the state comptroller, shall study and\nissue a comprehensive report on methods and opportunities to transition\nunbanked and underbanked state residents entitled to state government\npayments into the banking system.\n\nA comprehensive report summarizing all of the findings from subdivision\ntwo of this section shall be sent to the governor, the speaker of the\nassembly and the temporary president of the senate no later than eigh-\nteen months after the effective date of this act.\n\nSection 2. Provides that the act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany residents of New York have a difficult time with access to banking\nservices for their state government payments they may receive.\n\nThis bill would require a study to examine the types of payments made by\nstate agencies to state residents including but not limited to tax\nreturns, benefits, credits, unemployment insurance, and the lottery,\nwhich are made in a sufficient amount to the unbanked and underbanked.\nExamine what methods of payment are used by state agencies and whether\ndirect deposit and debit cards are included as options for recipients of\nsuch state payments. Determine whether opportunities exist when state\npayments are made to enable state agencies to provide financial educa-\ntion as it relates to the value of participation in the banking system.\n\nThe bill would also examine whether a contractual relationship with a\nminority bank or banks that define specific terms of financial service\nwould be an effective measure in encouraging use of direct deposit.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUse existing staff and resources of the department of financial and\nother cooperating state agencies.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
